453	\subsection{$b$-tagging}
454	\label{sec:b-tagging}
455	
456	Multiple $b$-tagging plays a central role since we require two $b$ jets from the boosted top decays and one or two additional $b$ jets from the spectator tops. Due to its large impact on the resulting sensitivity, a careful assessment on the $b$-tagging efficiency and associated jet-faking rate is required.
457	
458	In our semi-realistic $b$-tagging procedure, we assign a $b$-tag to each $r=0.4$ jet if there is a parton level $b$ or $c$ quark within $\Delta r=0.4$ from the jet axis, and we assume a $b$-tagging efficiency of
459	\begin{equation}
460		\epsilon_b = 0.70, \,\,\,\, \epsilon_c = 0.20, \,\,\,\,\, \epsilon_j = 0.01\,, 
461	\end{equation}
462	where $\epsilon_{b, c, j}$ are the efficiencies that a $b$, $c$ or a light jet will be tagged as a $b$-jet. 
463	We note that in recent ATLAS analysis (Ref. \cite{ATLAS13TeV}), the following b-tagging efficiencies are used
464	$\epsilon_b = 0.77$, $\epsilon_c = 0.22$, and $\epsilon_j = 0.0079$, which are slightly better than what we have used in our analysis.
465	
466	
467	For a fat jet to be $b$-tagged, we require that a $b$-tagged $r=0.4$ jet lands within $\Delta R = R$ from the fat jet axis, where $R$ is the size of a fat jet. We take into account that more than one $b$-jet might land inside the fat jet, whereby we reweigh at least $1b$-tagging efficiencies of a fat jet depending on the $b$-tagging scheme described in Table \ref{tab:btag}.
