992	The discovery potential of the $V_1$ resonance can be further improved by combining all three channels. In this section, we combine the results from the fully-hadronic, semi-leptonic and SSDL channels (each of which has a disjoint final state).
993	
994	In order to estimate the discovery reach, we define the significance $\sigma_{\rm dis}$ as a likelihood ratio~\cite{Cowan:2010js},
995	\beq
996		\sigma_{\rm dis} \equiv \sqrt{-2\, {\rm ln}\left( \frac{L(B | \mu S + B)}{L(\mu S + B| \mu S + B)} \right) }\,,
997	\eeq
998	where $S$ and $B$ are the expected number of signal and background events respectively, and $\mu$ denotes a signal modifier parameter relevant for reflecting correlated systematic uncertainties when combining different searches. 
999	Assuming all three channels are statistically independent, we use a combined likelihood given by the product of individual likelihoods
1000	\beq
1001		L(x |n) = \prod_{i=1}^N \frac{x_j^{n_j}}{n_j\!} e^{-x_j} \, ,
1002	\eeq
1003	where $i$ runs over the fully-hadronic, semi-leptonic and SSDL channels.
1004	
1005	Since correlated systematic uncertainties in three very different searches are unavailable for us,
1006	we simply take the signal modifier parameter $\mu = 1$, and for a discovery we demand
1007	\beq
1008		\sigma_{\rm dis} \geq 5.
1009	\eeq
1010	
1011	An exclusion limit, on the other hand, is estimated by using the likelihood ratio
1012	\beq
1013		\sigma_{\rm exc} \equiv \sqrt{-2\, {\rm ln}\left( \frac{L(\mu S + B|B)}{L(B|B)} \right) }\,,
1014	\eeq
1015	with a signal strength parameter $\mu = 1$. The 2$\sigma$ exclusion bound is obtained by
1016	\beq
1017		\sigma_{\rm exc} \geq 2.
1018	\eeq
1019	
1020	Figure \ref{fig:SigMap} summarizes the required luminosities in fb$^{-1}$ of the (upper-left panel) fully-hadronic, (upper-right panel) semi-leptonic, (lower-left panel) SSDL and (lower-right panel) combined channels for $5\sigma$ discovery at $\sqrt{s} = 14$ TeV LHC run II. For the very high luminosity of $\sim 3000 \; \rm fb^{-1}$ we can at most probe it down to $c_t \sim 1.6$ and $c_t \sim 2.9$ for $1.5 \TeV$ and $2.0 \TeV$ respectively in the combined channel. Since the significance isolines scale as the cross section (see Figure \ref{fig:4_top14}), it will be challenging to get any sensitivity in the $c_t < 1.0$ territory even during the high-luminosity phase of the LHC.
